Why the AGPL
------------
Two properties were wanted: every modified version stays under the same
licence, and nobody can take the work proprietary by offering it only as a
hosted service. Section 13 of the AGPL supplies the second one — anyone who
lets users interact with a modified version over a network must offer those
users its source under this same licence. A plain GPL would not: it is
triggered by distribution, and a web service distributes nothing.

Note that the AGPL does not forbid charging money, for hosting or otherwise.
No OSI-approved licence does; a non-commercial clause would make this project
not open source, cut it off from the scientific Python ecosystem, and bar
legitimate users such as companies doing internal research and universities
with commercial arms. What the AGPL guarantees instead is that a paid hosted
version cannot be a closed one: its users get the source, and any improvement
in it comes back to everyone under the same terms.
